    (v_topic_id, 'A 75-year-old patient presents with thigh pain 12 years after a total hip arthroplasty. Radiographs show eccentric wear of the polyethylene liner and focal osteolytic lesions around the acetabular and femoral components without signs of acute infection. What is the most likely underlying pathophysiologic mechanism?', '["Type IV hypersensitivity reaction to metal ions", "Low-grade chronic periprosthetic infection", "Stress shielding of the proximal femur", "Macrophage response to particulate wear debris", "Galvanic corrosion of the modular neck"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Particle disease (aseptic loosening) is caused by the generation of particulate wear debris (commonly polyethylene). Macrophages phagocytose this debris and release inflammatory cytokines (e.g., TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6), leading to increased osteoclast activity and subsequent osteolysis.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 79-year-old patient undergoes a total knee arthroplasty for a severe valgus deformity. Postoperatively, the patient has a foot drop and decreased sensation over the dorsum of the foot. Which nerve was most likely injured?', '["Saphenous nerve", "Tibial nerve", "Common peroneal nerve", "Deep femoral nerve", "Sural nerve"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Correction of a severe valgus deformity during TKA places the common peroneal nerve at risk of stretch injury. This presents clinically as a foot drop (loss of dorsiflexion and eversion) and numbness over the dorsum of the foot.'),
