    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old patient presents with increasing pain in the knee 3 weeks after a total knee arthroplasty. Aspiration yields purulent fluid. What is the most likely responsible organism and the standard surgical management?', '["Staphylococcus aureus; Two-stage exchange arthroplasty", "Staphylococcus epidermidis; Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR)", "Cutibacterium acnes; One-stage exchange arthroplasty", "Staphylococcus aureus; Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR)", "Streptococcus pyogenes; Suppressive intravenous antibiotics only"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Periprosthetic joint infections present differently based on timing. Acute infections (typically < 3-4 weeks) are often caused by highly virulent organisms like Staphylococcus aureus and can sometimes be managed with Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR). Chronic infections are usually due to less virulent organisms like Staph epidermidis and require a two-stage exchange.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old patient presents with increasing pain in the hip 8 months after a total hip arthroplasty. Aspiration yields purulent fluid. What is the most likely responsible organism and the standard surgical management?', '["Staphylococcus aureus; Two-stage exchange arthroplasty", "Staphylococcus epidermidis (Coagulase-negative Staph); Two-stage exchange arthroplasty", "Streptococcus pyogenes; Suppressive intravenous antibiotics only", "Cutibacterium acnes; One-stage exchange arthroplasty", "Staphylococcus epidermidis; Debridement, antibiotics, and implant retention (DAIR)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Periprosthetic joint infections present differently based on timing. Acute infections (typically < 3-4 weeks) are often caused by highly virulent organisms like Staphylococcus epidermidis (Coagulase-negative Staph) and can sometimes be managed with Two-stage exchange arthroplasty. Chronic infections are usually due to less virulent organisms like Staphylococcus epidermidis (Coagulase-negative Staph) and require a two-stage exchange.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 78-year-old male undergoes a total hip arthroplasty via a standard anterolateral approach. Postoperatively, the patient is at highest risk for dislocation if the hip is placed in which of the following positions?', '["Extension and internal rotation", "Extension and external rotation", "Flexion, adduction, and external rotation", "Flexion, abduction, and external rotation", "Extension, abduction, and internal rotation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In a anterolateral approach to the hip, the typical position of instability causing dislocation is extension and external rotation. The Superior gluteal nerve is also at risk during this approach. Patients are routinely placed on specific hip precautions to avoid these extreme ranges of motion early in the postoperative period.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 77-year-old patient is undergoing a total joint arthroplasty. The surgical team is reviewing the risk of perioperative systemic complications. Regarding wound dehiscence, which of the following is most accurate in this setting?', '["It only occurs after the 3-month postoperative mark", "It is a rare complication seen almost exclusively in young patients", "It is completely preventable with prophylactic antibiotics", "It is solely caused by the type of implant bearing surface used", "Associated with early excessive flexion or poor nutrition"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Wound dehiscence is a known systemic complication following major joint arthroplasty. Specifically, it is associated with early excessive flexion or poor nutrition. Proper medical optimization and perioperative care are crucial for risk mitigation.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 83-year-old sustains a fall and suffers a periprosthetic femur fracture around their total hip arthroplasty. Radiographs show the fracture is Around the stem, stem is well-fixed. This is classified as Vancouver B1. What is the most appropriate management?', '["ORIF with intramedullary nailing", "Revision arthroplasty with proximal femoral replacement", "Nonoperative management with protected weight-bearing", "ORIF with plates and cables", "Revision arthroplasty with a longer stem"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Vancouver classification dictates treatment of postoperative periprosthetic femur fractures. Vancouver B1 fractures occur Around the stem, stem is well-fixed and are best managed by ORIF with plates and cables.');
